{"label":"1A","direction":"against","claim":"Cancer/tumor-tagged GEO series are leaving the NovaSeq 6000 faster than non-cancer series, which contradicts a 'sticky clinical-adjacent tail' in public data.","detail":"Human GEO series released in Q3'24 → Q3'25 → Q3'26 (cancer OR tumor OR tumour): 6000 series 764 → 791 → 794; X series (GPL34281) 10 → 51 → 131. The 6000 share of 6000+X fell 98.7% → 93.9% → 85.8% (−12.9 pts). The non-cancer remainder (all minus cancer) fell 616/641 = 96.1% → 650/722 = 90.0% → 853/973 = 87.7% (−8.4 pts). Intermediate cancer points: Q1'26 859/947 = 90.7%, Q2'26 795/889 = 89.4%. Caveats: X count is the plain 'NovaSeq X' GPL only and excludes X Plus GPLs. The 'cancer' keyword matches mostly research series, and tagged series are ~49% of all 6000 series in every quarter.","url":"https://eutils.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/entrez/eutils/esearch.fcgi?db=gds&term=GPL24676[ACCN]+AND+gse[ETYP]+AND+(cancer+OR+tumor+OR+tumour)+AND+2026/07/01:2026/09/30[PDAT]&retmax=0","date":"2026-10-12 (data through Q3'26)","source_name":"NCBI GEO via E-utilities (db=gds), GPL24676 vs GPL34281","source_type":"dataset","quote":"","strength":"medium","why_new":"Known items give only all-series 6000 share; this splits by oncology keyword and by quarter, and shows no slower decay."},
{"label":"1A","direction":"against","claim":"Series that mention 'patient' or 'clinical' also show the 6000 share falling about 8 points in a year (93.5% → 85.5%), so there is no slower-decay clinical pocket.","detail":"Keyword (patient OR patients OR clinical): Q3'25 6000 = 608, X = 42, so the 6000 share is 608/650 = 93.5%. Q3'26 6000 = 597, X = 101, so the share is 597/698 = 85.5%. The all-series share over the same period went 92.1% → 86.8% (−5.3 pts), so the clinical-keyword group fell somewhat faster. The 6000's absolute count in this group was flat (−1.8%), while X series rose 2.4x.","url":"https://eutils.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/entrez/eutils/esearch.fcgi?db=gds&term=GPL24676[ACCN]+AND+gse[ETYP]+AND+(patient+OR+patients+OR+clinical)+AND+2026/07/01:2026/09/30[PDAT]&retmax=0","date":"2026-10-12","source_name":"NCBI GEO E-utilities","source_type":"dataset","quote":"","strength":"medium","why_new":"Adds a clinical-keyword cut and quantifies it against the all-series share."},
{"label":"1A","direction":"supports","claim":"Even in oncology-tagged public data, the 6000 still holds about 86% of series and its absolute count has not fallen, so the 6000 tail is not yet gone.","detail":"Cancer-tagged 6000 series were 764 (Q3'24), 791 (Q3'25) and 794 (Q3'26), roughly flat. Conversion is coming from X additions (10 → 131), not from the 6000 shrinking. Quarterly 6000 cancer series: Q1'26 859, Q2'26 795, Q3'26 794, a slight drift down from Q1. The 6000 therefore stays the majority platform in oncology-adjacent public work, though the mix share is falling about 4–5 pts per quarter-year-end.","url":"https://eutils.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/entrez/eutils/esearch.fcgi?db=gds&term=GPL24676[ACCN]+AND+gse[ETYP]+AND+(cancer+OR+tumor+OR+tumour)+AND+2026/01/01:2026/03/31[PDAT]&retmax=0","date":"2026-10-12","source_name":"NCBI GEO E-utilities","source_type":"dataset","quote":"","strength":"weak","why_new":"The oncology-tagged absolute 6000 series path (Q3'24–Q3'26) is new."},
